No Company shall directly/indirectly:
‐ give any loan to any person or other body corporate;
‐ give guarantee/security in connection with loan to any person/body corporate;
‐ acquire by way of subscription, purchase or otherwise, securities of any body corporate

exceeding 60% of paid‐up share capital, free reserves and securities premium account OR 100% of free reserves and
securities premium account, whichever is more

“Related Party Transactions (RPTs)”. Its purpose is to ensure that transactions between a company and its related parties are properly authorised, conducted on appropriate terms, and disclosed.

Related party”, with reference to a company, means—

(i) a director or his relative;

(ii) a key managerial personnel or his relative;

(iii) a firm, in which a director, manager or his relative is a partner;

(iv) a private company in which a director or manager or his relative is a member or director;

(v) a public company in which a director or manager is a director and holds along with his relatives, more than two per cent. of its paid-up share capital;

(vi) any body corporate whose Board of directors, managing director or manager is accustomed to act in accordance with the advice, directions or instructions of a director or manager;

(vii) any person under whose advice, directions or instructions a director or manager is accustomed to act:

Provided that nothing in sub-clauses (vi) and (vii) shall apply to the advice, directions or instructions given in a professional capacity;

(viii) any body corporate which is—

a holding, subsidiary or an associate company of such company; or
a subsidiary of a holding company to which it is also a subsidiary;

(ix) an investing company or the venturer of the company;

Explanation.— For the purposes of this clause, “the investing company or the venturer of a company” means a body corporate whose investment in the company would result in the company becoming an associate company of the body corporate.

(x) such other person as may be prescribed.

Applicable to an Indian company establishing an overseas subsidiary/WOS, JV, or making any Overseas Direct Investment (ODI)

ODI compliance generally arises where an Indian company:

-incorporates or acquires an overseas Wholly Owned Subsidiary (WOS);
-acquires shares/control in an overseas Joint Venture (JV);
-makes further equity investment in an existing overseas subsidiary/JV;
-provides a loan/debt investment to the overseas entity;
-gives a corporate/performance guarantee or other permitted non-fund-based financial commitment;
-creates a pledge/charge in connection with permitted overseas financial commitment; or
-undertakes certain other transactions which qualify as financial commitment/ODI.

penalty:
Where the amount involved is quantifiable: penalty of up to 3 times the amount involved;

Where the amount is not quantifiable: penalty of up to Rs. 2 lakh; and

For a continuing contravention: an additional penalty of up to Rs. 5,000 per day after the first day

brief_description:
When an Indian company issues equity shares/equity instruments to a non-resident investor, it falls under the FDI/Foreign Investment regime under FEMA

This compliance applies where an Indian company receives investment from a person resident outside India by way of:

i) issue of equity shares;
-partly paid equity shares;
-warrants;
-other permitted equity instruments;
ii) issue under rights/bonus, where the investor is a non-resident;
iii)conversion of permitted instruments into equity;
iv)issue pursuant to a merger/demerger/restructuring, where applicable.
